00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,880
 Now, one of the things that was quite interesting is we in the academic field had the right

2
00:00:03,880 --> 00:00:08,520
 to use this network, but when it first started out, it was supposed to be academic only.

3
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:13,400
 And then once it was established in the late '80s and early '90s, there were some people

4
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:17,960
 that started sort of like bending those rules or bypassing those rules or ignoring those

5
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:18,960
 rules.

6
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:21,720
 And it wasn't really clear who was supposed to police those rules.

7
00:00:21,720 --> 00:00:27,000
 And the people like at the University of Michigan, their heart wasn't in reducing who would use

8
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:28,000
 the Internet.

9
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:29,640
 They wanted to expand, right?

10
00:00:29,640 --> 00:00:34,640
 And so there were places, often bulletin board systems would somehow get a connection to

11
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:39,400
 the Internet, and then they would give Internet access to a bunch of non-academics.

12
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:47,100
 And so it became increasingly in the late '80s understood by everyone around the world.

13
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:48,760
 But this is pretty much nerds, right?

14
00:00:48,760 --> 00:00:54,240
 They can sit with a screen, with a command line interface, with a text-based screen,

15
00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:56,360
 and they can get excited about that.

16
00:00:56,360 --> 00:00:57,360
 I'm like that.

17
00:00:57,360 --> 00:01:02,280
 I get excited about things that are non-graphical because I just make it up in my head.

18
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:08,720
 But we needed something to pop this into the collective consciousness of all humanity rather

19
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:13,800
 than just academics first and then nerds second.

20
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:16,040
 But that was about to change.

