 We went to the University of Michigan.

23
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:23,440
 We got the NSF net funded.

24
00:01:23,440 --> 00:01:25,920
 And now we are going to fly across the ocean to CERN.

25
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:30,680
 CERN, of course, is a high energy physics laboratory.

26
00:01:30,680 --> 00:01:36,960
 They do this 26-mile circle and smash particles and take pictures of the smashing particles

27
00:01:36,960 --> 00:01:42,200
 and looking for the Higgs boson or found the Higgs boson.

28
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:44,960
 It's a really cool place I recommend that you visit.

29
00:01:44,960 --> 00:01:52,320
 The CERN is a place that physicists from all over the world visit, live at, and collaborate

30
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:53,440
 with.

31
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:58,160
 For experimental physics, it really revolves around those experimental facilities.

32
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:03,400
 And so regardless of whether you live in Russia or Australia or Germany or America or Japan,

33
00:02:03,400 --> 00:02:08,280
 if you're a high energy nuclear physicist, you have got to work with CERN or spend time

34
00:02:08,280 --> 00:02:09,320
 at CERN.

35
00:02:09,320 --> 00:02:13,280
 And those who spend time at CERN tend to make the best discoveries.

36
00:02:13,280 --> 00:02:17,400
 And so these projects have such long lead times, and they take so many different kinds

37
00:02:17,400 --> 00:02:18,400
 of talented people.

38
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:24,880
 There are metallurgy, welders, physicists, engineers, designers, project managers.

39
00:02:24,880 --> 00:02:26,600
 There's a ton of people involved in it.

40
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:29,720
 And it's a pretty well-funded operation, and everyone's pretty smart.

41
